==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|domes}} + {{m|enm|dai}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|dom||judgment}} + {{m|ang|dæg||day}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈduːmz.deɪ/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-doomsday.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|dooms|day|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Concerned with or predicting future [[universal]] [[destruction]].
# Given to or marked by [[foreboding]]s or [[prediction]]s of impending [[calamity]].
# Capable of causing [[widespread]] or [[total]] destruction.

====Derived terms====
{{rel-top3|Terms derived from the adjective "doomsday"}}
* [[doomsday bomb]]
* [[Domesday Book]]
{{rel-mid3}}
* [[doomsday event]]
* [[doomsday machine]]
{{rel-mid3}}
* [[doomsday scenario]]
* [[doomsday weapon]]
{{rel-bottom}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The day when [[God]] is expected to judge the [[world]]; [[end times]].
# [[judgement day]]; the day of the [[Final Judgment]]; any day of decisive judgement or final dissolution.

====Synonyms====
* [[apocalypse]]
* [[Armageddon]]
* [[end times]]
* [[Final Judgment]]
* [[judgement day]]
* [[nuclear holocaust]]
* [[Ragnarok]] or [[Ragnarök]]

